MM1 -- the seven MM/1 system modules in CMDS/MM1The MM/1 was an OS-9/68000 personal computer, and these are modules forits own hardware: the keyboard, the mouse, the sound chip, the floppycontroller, the SCSI bus and the K-Windows display.  They are not programs.Each one goes into an MM/1's boot file, or is loaded before the device thatuses it is first opened; on any other machine there is nothing for them todrive.  They came together in one archive, updates.lzh, as newer editionsof modules an MM/1 already has.What each one is, read from its module header:  file               module      type                 edition   bytes  windio.52          windio      device driver ($0E)       52   32798  keydrv.mm1         keydrv      system module ($0C)       46    1474  msdrv.901_340      msdrv       system module ($0C)        8     656  msdrv_340.901.ms   msdrv       system module ($0C)        8     674  snddrv             snddrv      system module ($0C)        4    1836  rb37c65            rb37c65     device driver ($0E)        7    2592  scsi_mm1a          scsi_mm1a   subroutine ($02)          28    4218  windio      the K-Windows terminal driver: the MM/1's windowing console,              windows, graphics and fonts, reached through the /w devices.  keydrv      the keyboard's scan-code handler that windio calls; its              tables hold the US layout.  msdrv       the mouse handler windio calls.  Two builds of edition 8,              different in size, and both register the name msdrv, so              only one can be in memory at a time; the archive does not              say which suits which machine, beyond the file names.  snddrv      the sound handler.  rb37c65     the RBF driver for the 37C65 floppy disk controller.  scsi_mm1a   the low-level SCSI routines the MM/1's hard disk driver              links to, not a driver on its own.Installing one: keep a copy of the boot file that works, merge the newmodule into a new boot file in place of the old one with `os9gen', andboot from it.  A module that is already in memory is replaced by a neweredition only when the old one is unlinked, which for the console and thedisks means at the next boot.